Ramp On
The ramp on and off functions use a Gaussian profile to reach peak amplitude. For example, the ramp_on function is
\[
\Omega(t) =
\begin{cases}
\Omega_0 \exp \left[ -4 \log 2 \left( \frac{t - t_0}{t_w}
\right)^2 \right] & t &lt; t_0\\
\Omega_0 & t \ge t_0
\end{cases}
\]
where \(t_0\) (centre_1) is the point at which the function reaches its peak amplitude \(\Omega_0\) (ampl_1). The duration of the ramp-on is governed by \(t_w\) (fwhm_1), which is the full width at half maximum (FWHM) of a Gaussian. The ramp_off, ramp_onoff and ramp_offon functions behave in the same way.

